\description{
Pools a dose-response relationship across studies. Give it one long table,
one row per exposure category per study, say which summary measure the
studies report, and name the shape you want fitted. It returns the pooled
dose-response, its heterogeneity, the prediction interval, the predicted
effects, a viability check on the studies, and a name for the curve.

The arguments are the ones a \code{meta} user already knows: \code{sm}, \code{studlab},
\code{event.e}, \code{n.e}, \code{TE}, \code{lower}, \code{upper}, \code{method.tau}, \code{level},
\code{common} and \code{random}. You write \code{sm = "RR"} where another package would
ask for \code{logrr ~ dose}, and easyDR derives the contrasts, the standard
errors and the within-study covariances from your table.
}
\section{Which measure, and so which columns}{

\code{sm} decides what kind of outcome this is and therefore which columns are
read.\tabular{lll}{
   \code{sm} \tab Outcome \tab Columns read \cr
   \code{"RR"} \tab risk ratio \tab \code{event.e}, \code{n.e}, or \code{TE} with \code{lower}/\code{upper} \cr
   \code{"OR"} \tab odds ratio \tab the same, from a case-control design \cr
   \code{"IRR"} \tab incidence rate ratio \tab \code{event.e} with person-time in \code{n.e} \cr
   \code{"MD"} \tab mean difference \tab \code{mean.e}, \code{sd.e}, \code{n.e} \cr
   \code{"SMD"} \tab standardised mean difference \tab \code{mean.e}, \code{sd.e}, \code{n.e} \cr
}


Column names are guessed from the table when you leave them \code{NULL}, and the
guesses are printed. Naming them is always allowed and never penalised.
